    810	| S193 commit-thread step 1 (Connes amortisation) — adversarial re-examination of S53's E3.1 closure: does S53 argument 2 (O(K^3) per-query diagonalisation cost) survive the AMORTISATION challenge of "diagonalise once, serve many π(x) queries"? | REFINEMENT+CLOSURE | E | Thread 2 commit step 1/5. **Adversarial finding**: the S53 closure of E3.1 stands but its dominant argument is sharpened. Argument 2 partially collapses — under amortisation across Q queries up to x=X, per-query cost reduces to O(K) explicit-formula evaluation, paid once-O(K³) for setup. **However**, Galway 2004 + Hiary computes the SAME K zeros at setup cost O(K^{17/13}) (Hiary t^{4/13}+ε per zero, height t≈K), with identical per-query O(K). **Setup ratio K³/K^{17/13} = K^{22/13} ≈ K^{1.69}**: at x = 10^{100}, Connes setup is 10^{84.6} times more expensive than Galway. Connes is strictly dominated by Galway in every cost regime — per-query (O(K) vs O(K), tie), one-shot (O(K³) vs O(K^{17/13}), Galway wins), full amortisation (K³/Q + K vs K^{17/13}/Q + K, Galway wins). **Empirical confirmation**: K_sustained(x) on x ∈ {100, 1000, 5000, 50000} fits to K_sust(x) ~ 0.48 · x^{0.55} (predicted slope 0.5, agrees with E1.5 / Riemann-von Mangoldt floor). **Reduction**: Thread 2 (Connes amortisation) ⊆ Thread 3 (Galway frontier). The Connes operator is just an alternative (and strictly more expensive) way to obtain the same K zeros that Galway/Hiary obtain more cheaply; per-query costs are identical because both routes reduce to the same explicit-formula partial sum on the same K zeros. **Sharper closure of E3.1 (refines row 706)**: even granting full amortisation and CCM unlimited per-prime accuracy, the diagonalisation setup cost K³ is dominated by Hiary's K^{17/13} for getting the same K zeros — independent of per-prime accuracy claims, of operator construction details, of amortisation regime. The remaining lever is K(x) = polylog(x) "in distribution" (Thread 3), to which both routes are equally subject. Cites E3.1, E1.5, S53 row 706, Galway 2004, Hiary 2011 (state_of_art_2026.md §2.5b). Cross-domain ingredient: classical asymmetric setup-cost comparison between dense eigensolvers (O(K³)) and Hiary's specialised zero-locating algorithm (O(t^{4/13}) per zero) — mildly novel framing; S53 had not compared to the Hiary baseline. See experiments/analytic/connes_amortisation/. | 193 |
